WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.200
Mark Zuckerberg is on a VR spending spree. “I&nbsp;
don’t know if folks realize… Facebook’s largest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:10.320
expenditure for multiple years now has been VR.”&nbsp;
We don’t know exactly how much they are spending,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:15.920
but most people think it’s around $5 billion per&nbsp;
year. And that’s after they spent $2 billion to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:22.640
buy Oculus in 2014. It’s now 7 years later and VR&nbsp;
still hasn’t reached mass adoption. “The average&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:29.360
number of times people use a virtual reality&nbsp;
headset is one” VR is an amazing technology,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:34.000
but it just hasn’t gotten to the point where&nbsp;
average people can enjoy it every day. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:39.520
Zuckerberg isn’t taking his foot off the gas. He&nbsp;
now has 20% of Facebook’s employees working on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:44.000
VR in some capacity. It’s a massive investment&nbsp;
that sounds way out of line with the current&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:49.760
market opportunity. Both Oculus and Instagram were&nbsp;
acquired around the same time for about the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:55.600
price, but Instagram brings in more than 10 times&nbsp;
as much money for Facebook as Oculus and at a much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.600 --> 00:01:01.680
higher margin. So why is Zuck still so obsessed&nbsp;
with VR? Well, there are a few different reasons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:07.040
but to understand them, we need to talk about&nbsp;
the metaverse. So put lets on our VR headsets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:11.200
and dive in. “So what is the metaverse? It's&nbsp;
a virtual environment where you can be present&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:14.960
with people in digital spaces. You can kind&nbsp;
of think about this as an embodied internet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:19.200
that you're inside of rather than just looking&nbsp;
at. We believe that this is going to be the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:25.280
successor to the mobile internet.” For the past&nbsp;
few months, everyone in tech has been talking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:29.760
about the metaverse non-stop. There are lots of&nbsp;
different definitions floating around out there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:34.640
but no one has done more work to popularize the&nbsp;
term Metaverse than Matthew Ball: “The best way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:39.200
to think about it is as a quasi-successor state&nbsp;
to today’s mobile internet, in much the same way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:44.960
today’s mobile internet built off the internet of&nbsp;
the 90s.” Lots of people, especially engineers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:49.600
are extremely skeptical when a business blogger&nbsp;
comes along and coins a phrase for a vague&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:55.120
collection of technology trends, but sometimes&nbsp;
these terms do have a practical use. Think about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.120 --> 00:02:00.720
how we label generations. Terms like boomer,&nbsp;
millennial, and gen z are all loosely defined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:05.840
around a period of dates we can’t all completely&nbsp;
agree upon. People typically use the birth years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:14.160
between 1981 to 1996 to define the millennial&nbsp;
generation, but is someone born on January 1st of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:20.720
1997 all that different? Obviously not. But that&nbsp;
doesn’t mean there isn’t value in having a term&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:26.640
to describe a new era. Technology has been through&nbsp;
a few of these, and sometimes they are only clear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:32.000
in hindsight. After mainframe computers were&nbsp;
adopted by corporations, the personal computing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:36.880
era brought technology into the homes of millions&nbsp;
of people. It wasn’t long before these computers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:41.680
were connected to the internet, which marked&nbsp;
the beginning of the dot com era, and the last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:47.760
15 years have clearly been defined by the mobile&nbsp;
phone. Each era of computing has had huge winners,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:53.680
but no one company can bring about a new era&nbsp;
alone. And that’s why the metaverse is a useful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:59.760
concept. It’s not just “Fortnite”, or “VR”, or&nbsp;
“crypto”, the metaverse is an idea that ties these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:05.600
concepts together and seeks to explain how they&nbsp;
all interconnect. So, you’re probably wondering,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:10.320
how will the metaverse be any different from&nbsp;
today’s internet? Fortunately, Matthew Ball has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:14.960
laid out a few key ideas that highlight what&nbsp;
this evolved form of the internet will look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:20.720
like. First, the metaverse will be persistent,&nbsp;
meaning that it never resets, pauses, or ends.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:25.280
Massively multiplayer online role playing games&nbsp;
are the closest thing we have to this currently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:30.160
but not many other online experiences replicate&nbsp;
this well. When you win a match in Fortnite,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:36.160
the map resets entirely, and anything you built&nbsp;
in the game is gone forever. Second, the metaverse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:41.680
will be live. Much of the modern internet is built&nbsp;
for asynchronous use. You can watch this YouTube&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:46.880
video at any time for example. And pre-scheduled&nbsp;
events will take place in the metaverse for sure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:51.920
but everything will be designed to be live&nbsp;
by default. Third, experiences will span both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:57.520
digital and physical worlds. Pokémon Go was the&nbsp;
first massively adopted augmented reality game&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.520 --> 00:04:02.240
and that gives us a preview of what is to come.&nbsp;
This theme isn’t restricted to gaming specifically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:07.280
though. Microsoft is already helping companies&nbsp;
build “digital twins” that represent physical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:31.760
infrastructure in virtual spaces. ELABORATE HERE&nbsp;
Fourth, there will be no cap on concurrent users.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:35.200
This is one of the hardest problems to&nbsp;
solve from a technical point of view,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:41.200
but it’s critical. The biggest online games still&nbsp;
restrict each session to around 100 players. Even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:46.880
though millions might be playing at a given time&nbsp;
worldwide, a single server will be capped. Massive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.880 --> 00:04:51.840
concurrency will make metaverse experiences feel&nbsp;
very different from the present-day internet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:56.960
Fifth, the metaverse will have a fully functioning&nbsp;
economy. Increasingly, the internet is simply the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.960 --> 00:05:01.440
best place to make money. Nearly every business&nbsp;
has moved online, but there’s still a long way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:07.680
to go until we have a digitally native economy.&nbsp;
Most transactions are siloed in particular virtual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:12.800
spaces and investment in digital goods is still&nbsp;
in its infancy. Sixth, the metaverse will have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:17.760
unprecedented interoperability. This means being&nbsp;
able to bring a Fortnite skin into Minecraft and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:22.640
vice versa. Tim Sweeney, who founded Epic Games&nbsp;
and owns Fortnite has been talking about this for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:27.040
years now, and it’s clearly an important goal.&nbsp;
These days, most internet products are walled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:32.400
gardens and users have no real way of moving&nbsp;
data, items, content, or currencies between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:37.840
services. Lastly, user generated content will play&nbsp;
a massive role in the metaverse. This is basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:43.040
a requirement, since no single company could ever&nbsp;
hope to produce enough variation in their games to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.040 --> 00:05:47.760
satisfy everyone. Roblox and Minecraft have been&nbsp;
so successful because community members create&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:52.480
engaging and unique experiences for each other.&nbsp;
Now, you’re probably thinking that those are all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.480 --> 00:05:56.880
pretty incremental developments, and they are!&nbsp;
Individually, none of them are going to really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.880 --> 00:06:01.600
change anything about how we experience modern&nbsp;
technology, but taken together, they can create&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:06.800
some pretty remarkable things. When multiple,&nbsp;
loosely related technologies start to get better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:12.080
at the same time, there are often surprising&nbsp;
second-order effects. And we’ve seen this exact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.080 --> 00:06:17.360
thing happen several times in the past. Consider&nbsp;
the second industrial revolution that occurred in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:22.640
the late 19th century. Thomas Edison built the&nbsp;
first electric power stations in 1881, but it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.640 --> 00:06:27.040
wasn’t until 40 years later that electricity&nbsp;
really started being used to power machines.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.040 --> 00:06:30.800
That’s because factories weren’t designed&nbsp;
around electricity when it became available.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:35.440
Most plants just replaced their steam generators&nbsp;
with electrical ones and called it a day. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.440 --> 00:06:39.760
failed to take advantage of the full benefit of&nbsp;
electrification. Older factories were designed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.760 --> 00:06:44.160
to have a single power source that was either&nbsp;
completely on, or completely off. This meant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:49.040
that when the factory was on, every machine was&nbsp;
running, but electricity can be routed locally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.040 --> 00:06:54.400
and turned on and off based on the specific&nbsp;
needs of the moment. Eventually, new factories&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:59.360
were built to take advantage of the true value of&nbsp;
electricity, and productivity soared. Henry Ford&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.360 --> 00:07:04.720
built the first moving assembly line which cut&nbsp;
the production time for a single car from over 12&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.720 --> 00:07:10.080
hours to just 93 minutes. This was only possible&nbsp;
because electricity was available everywhere&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:14.720
throughout the plant and the factory could be&nbsp;
designed for maximum efficiency. We tend to think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.720 --> 00:07:20.160
of Thomas Edison as single-handedly bringing about&nbsp;
the electrification of America. It was literally a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:24.640
“lightbulb moment”. But that narrative completely&nbsp;
ignores the advances in power management,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.640 --> 00:07:28.880
manufacturing hardware, and production&nbsp;
theory that collectively led to the real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.880 --> 00:07:35.040
impact of electrification. That’s why treating the&nbsp;
metaverse as “just VR” is so wrong. VR might be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:40.720
foundational metaverse technology, but as we’ve&nbsp;
seen, it’s clearly not enough. We’ve had decent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.720 --> 00:07:46.160
VR headsets for years now, but no metaverse. If&nbsp;
Thomas Edison is a little old school for you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.160 --> 00:07:51.120
don’t worry, we can learn all the same lessons&nbsp;
from Steve Jobs and the iPhone. Most people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:55.840
will point to the iPhone as the beginning of the&nbsp;
mobile internet era, and just like the lightbulb,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.840 --> 00:08:01.280
they aren’t wrong. But the full story is much&nbsp;
more complex. Even though the first iPhone did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.280 --> 00:08:07.440
have a web browser, it didn’t have a 3G modem, and&nbsp;
most websites had yet to be optimized for mobile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.440 --> 00:08:12.080
so the experience was pretty bad. It wasn’t until&nbsp;
the second iPhone that the mobile internet really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.080 --> 00:08:16.880
started taking off, and even then, this&nbsp;
was almost a full decade after the wireless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.880 --> 00:08:21.520
application protocol created the standards&nbsp;
that allowed phones to access the internet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.400 --> 00:08:26.800
Obviously there were lots of internet-connected&nbsp;
mobile devices before the iPhone, and the impact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.800 --> 00:08:31.440
of mobile wasn’t entirely clear until apps like&nbsp;
Instagram and Uber started creating businesses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.440 --> 00:08:35.680
that couldn’t exist before. The idea of a&nbsp;
supercomputer in your pocket has been alluring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.680 --> 00:08:42.320
for decades. In 1976, the sci-fi author Arthur C.&nbsp;
Clarke published “Imperial Earth.” In the book,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.320 --> 00:08:47.920
he described a mobile video phone called the&nbsp;
“minisec” which had global data connectivity and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.920 --> 00:08:52.800
sounds an awful lot like the iPhone. And in the&nbsp;
90s a team of engineers spun out of Apple to start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.800 --> 00:08:57.760
a new company called “General Magic”. The goal of&nbsp;
that company was to basically create an iPhone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.760 --> 00:09:01.840
but they had to build everything from scratch&nbsp;
and went bankrupt before launching it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.480 --> 00:09:07.760
The iPhone was only possible because so many&nbsp;
other technological visions had finally become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.760 --> 00:09:12.880
realities. Steve Jobs deserves all the credit in&nbsp;
the world for the iPhone, but it was only possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.880 --> 00:09:18.480
because other entrepreneurs had built foundational&nbsp;
technologies. Screen manufacturers made the iPhone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.480 --> 00:09:23.600
durable. Chip designers made the iPhone powerful.&nbsp;
And app developers made the iPhone useful. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.600 --> 00:09:28.560
iPhone was the culmination of dozens of different&nbsp;
independent technologies, working together to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:34.080
produce a truly revolutionary product. Investors&nbsp;
often talk about the “iPhone moment” as a key&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.080 --> 00:09:38.400
turning point of any new technology. It’s the&nbsp;
point where everything comes together and really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.400 --> 00:09:44.080
starts working and it often marks the dawn of a&nbsp;
new era. In some ways, the “lightbulb moment” and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.080 --> 00:09:48.800
the “iPhone moment” couldn’t be more different.&nbsp;
The lightbulb was merely an idea that sparked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.800 --> 00:09:53.760
decades of innovation, but didn’t have immediate&nbsp;
consumer value. The iPhone on the other hand was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.760 --> 00:09:59.440
not just a single idea, it was the synthesis of&nbsp;
dozens of technological trends. And that’s how you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.440 --> 00:10:04.240
should think about the metaverse. It certainly&nbsp;
hasn’t had an iPhone moment yet, but there may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.240 --> 00:10:09.200
have been a lightbulb moment, even though we can’t&nbsp;
quite put our finger on it yet. In a few years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.200 --> 00:10:14.000
we might point to Oculus, Fortnite, or Ethereum&nbsp;
as the true beginning of the metaverse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:18.240
but that won’t matter as much as what comes&nbsp;
next. The iPhone moment for the metaverse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.240 --> 00:10:23.120
will only come after we have made significant&nbsp;
progress in several key areas of technology.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.120 --> 00:10:28.160
Computers need to get faster in order to support&nbsp;
more realistic rendering and physics calculations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.160 --> 00:10:32.640
It’s no coincidence that the biggest games&nbsp;
all have the most simplistic graphics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.640 --> 00:10:37.840
Fortnite, Roblox, and Minecraft are all&nbsp;
designed to run on basically any device,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.840 --> 00:10:41.920
so they have to be simple. Internet connections&nbsp;
also need to get a lot stronger for the metaverse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.920 --> 00:10:46.480
to really work. Netflix is able to stream you&nbsp;
HD video content because they can compress&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.480 --> 00:10:50.880
the footage you're watching in advance and&nbsp;
store the file on a server in your region.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.880 --> 00:10:55.600
We’ve all seen how difficult it is to maintain&nbsp;
a stable connection even on a basic video call,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.600 --> 00:11:01.440
and I wouldn’t call zoom an immersive experience.&nbsp;
Most video calls are 720p, highly compressed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.440 --> 00:11:08.000
and 24 frames per second at best. In order for VR&nbsp;
to feel immersive, you usually need two separate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:14.240
streams of near 4k video running at 120 frames&nbsp;
per second. If internet connections get faster,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.240 --> 00:11:18.800
the content you’re viewing in VR could be&nbsp;
rendered on a server and then streamed to you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.800 --> 00:11:23.440
like how Google’s Stadia runs games in the cloud&nbsp;
and just sends you the resulting video feed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.440 --> 00:11:28.800
So hardware will need to get better and smaller,&nbsp;
but that might actually be the easy part. See,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.800 --> 00:11:33.760
computers basically get better every year. But&nbsp;
the networks built on top of those computers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.760 --> 00:11:38.480
only get more complicated, intertwined, and&nbsp;
entrenched. The real promise of the metaverse is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.480 --> 00:11:44.960
one of universality. It’s right there in the name.&nbsp;
The verse in metaverse only works if we develop a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.960 --> 00:11:50.080
full-fledged world that is open to anyone and&nbsp;
encompases everything. Tech companies are in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.080 --> 00:11:54.800
an interesting position right now. They want to&nbsp;
help the metaverse become a reality, but only as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.800 --> 00:11:59.440
long as it doesn’t challenge their dominance. The&nbsp;
reason that Zuckerberg is investing so heavily in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.440 --> 00:12:05.520
the metaverse right now is because Facebook has by&nbsp;
far the largest user base of any tech company. 3.5&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.520 --> 00:12:10.720
billion people already use Facebook, that’s half&nbsp;
of everyone on Earth. And all those people are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.720 --> 00:12:15.280
creating and consuming content through Facebook&nbsp;
apps already. This is a massive head start for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.280 --> 00:12:20.080
Facebook, which is why Zuckerberg is happy to&nbsp;
invest billions of dollars to make sure Facebook&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.080 --> 00:12:25.200
maintains a dominant position in what comes next.&nbsp;
But Facebook has stiff competition. One of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.200 --> 00:12:30.400
cornerstones of the metaverse will undoubtedly&nbsp;
be payments infrastructure. The ability to buy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.400 --> 00:12:35.440
sell, and most importantly own things in the&nbsp;
metaverse could be the most transformative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.440 --> 00:12:38.960
part of this new era. Facebook hasn’t&nbsp;
really been able to crack payments yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.960 --> 00:12:44.880
but crypto certainly has. The biggest gripe most&nbsp;
people have with Facebook is that it’s essentially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.880 --> 00:12:49.360
controlled by a single person. Crypto has a lot&nbsp;
of work to do if the plan is to defeat Facebook,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.360 --> 00:12:54.000
but metaverse-like projects are already starting&nbsp;
to gain traction, although they are extremely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.000 --> 00:12:58.720
small at the moment. Even though metaverse-related&nbsp;
crypto projects are still in their infancy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.720 --> 00:13:03.520
it’s totally possible that one of these projects&nbsp;
winds up being the biggest winner, instead of an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.520 --> 00:13:08.400
existing tech company. As we saw with the mobile&nbsp;
internet era, there were tons of big winners,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.400 --> 00:13:13.520
but Apple was probably the biggest winner of all.&nbsp;
Even though Apple had been around for decades,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.520 --> 00:13:18.080
they were in the right place at the right time&nbsp;
and executed perfectly. Facebook wants to build&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.080 --> 00:13:22.480
something so fundamental to the metaverse that&nbsp;
they can capture that iPhone moment. And if they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.480 --> 00:13:27.200
succeed, the billions they’ve invested will look&nbsp;
like pennies. All of this barely scratches the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.200 --> 00:13:33.840
surface of what’s going on with the metaverse, so&nbsp;
subscribe for future videos. Thanks for watching.

